WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.670
This is the debrief for Works On My Machine, where a fragile report has to give identical results in any session.

2
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.470
First, find files from the argument, not the working directory.

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.690
Second, depend on nothing in the session: no attached packages and no options.

4
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.710
Third, record exactly what the report ran on.

5
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:29.130
Line 2 builds the path from the data_dir argument with file.path(), so the working directory no longer matters.

6
00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.900
Every call outside base R carries its package prefix, so nothing depends on what happens to be attached.

7
00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:39.560
Missing outcomes are dropped first, so n counts only real values, and each statistic is computed per arm.

8
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:45.080
The label uses sprintf(), which always prints a decimal point, unlike format(), which follows options(OutDec).

9
00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:50.000
The function returns the table together with a session record.

10
00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.340
It records the R version and the exact dplyr version.

11
00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:59.340
And it stores an MD5 checksum of the input file, so anyone can confirm the same data went in.

12
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.590
Three mistakes account for most failed attempts.

13
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:09.230
Reading "visits.csv" by its bare name only works from one folder, and the wd check fails.

14
00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:14.650
Building the label with format() changes the decimal mark under different options, and the options check fails.

15
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:21.010
And changing options() or setwd() without restoring them breaks the caller's session, which fails the side-effects check.

16
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.100
This solution passes all seven checks.

17
00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:31.100
The validator self-test runs this exact code against every one of them, so what you see here is what the boss grades.

18
00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.080
Your own solution can look different.

19
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.460
What matters is that it passes the same checks.
